@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — where the failed run goes
#
# One generalization, formed on 2026-09-10 after the external alchemy was
# opened and printed its own excusing clause as part of the recipe. The
# holding had carried the same form in five further transmissions, entered one
# at a time over months and never read against one another.
#

pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The failed run is assigned somewhere other than to the operation"@en, "Der misslungene Durchgang wird anderswohin zugewiesen als an die Operation"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission prescribes an operation whose success cannot be established apart from the operation itself, the same transmission supplies a condition under which a run that produced nothing counts as no failure of the operation, and it locates that condition in the operator, in the person operated on, in the material, or in a third party, rather than in an outcome that a further run could establish."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:JinchengYaocheng, pmc:Shenbushi, pmc:SanjiLiuxiong, pmc:FailureIsSulkinessOrLostPower, pmc:BengeFailureCauses, pmc:AmuletFailureIsAlwaysExcused, pmc:ShangqingShijieZhengbian, pmc:NeidanFeidaofuren ;
    skos:note "An eighth witness was added on 2026-09-11 and it is the only one that states the rule instead of instancing it. The internal alchemy puts the practitioner's question — he has held to the work and seen no result — at the head of its chapter on the signs of verification, and answers it twice in four characters: it is not that the Way has failed the man. In its place it supplies two conditions, both in the operator, an unenlightened master and ignorance of the times. Where the other seven have to be read off a doctrine, this one is the doctrine, and it names the possibility it excludes."@en ;
    skos:note "A seventh witness was added on 2026-09-11 and it is the limiting case of the statement. Where the others supply a condition under which a run that produced nothing counts as no failure, the Zhen'gao supplies a rule of reading under which the outcome that would end the practitioner's participation altogether — his death — is classified as the attainment, and it does so for every state a corpse can be found in, the intact and the putrefied alike. The condition has there been moved from the run to the observation, which is one step further than any of the six."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "What each witness supplies, in the transmission's own terms. The golden elixir: where the assay yields no gold the drug is to be sealed and fired again for the same count of days, and then it never fails to form; and where it still does not form, an unbeliever may have slandered it. The medical canon: where the treatment establishes nothing, the spirit does not lend itself, and the passage supplies no mark by which the absence could have been seen beforehand. The burial book: the commentary on the sixth adversity enumerates the ways in which nothing happens, and every one of them is a circumstance of the family rather than of the site. The Kongo corpus: a charged thing that fails is either sulky and wants a sacrifice or has lost its power and wants renewing, and both branches cost a fee. The Zande oracle: eight named causes for a self-contradiction, from the wrong variety of creeper to the anger of the ghosts, and the recorder's summary that the errors as well as the sound judgements are taken as proof of its accuracy. The Slave Coast amulet: where the thing fails at the single danger it was made against, they are never at a loss for an excuse."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is about where the failure is put and not about whether the operations work. A transmission that supplies such a clause is not thereby making a false claim; it is making one that the ordinary check cannot reach, because the check's negative outcome has been given a home in advance. That is the difference this project exists to record, and it is a property of the wording rather than of the world."@en ;
    skos:note "The witnesses are not of equal standing and the difference runs along one line. In the three Chinese cases the excusing clause is printed by the transmission itself, as part of the instruction or of the commentary that travels with it: nobody is being answered, and no outside observer is involved. In the three African cases it is the report of a European recorder, and two of the three recorders say so in their own voice — one holds that the practitioners themselves are responsible for the prohibitions because a prohibition leaves a reasonable excuse, and another summarises an attitude rather than quoting an informant. Observers of that period were looking for exactly this shape. The generalization therefore rests on the Chinese witnesses, where the clause is self-attested, and is corroborated with that caveat by the others."@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search, carried against this holding before the statement was written, as SPEC §16 requires. Two near-misses and no refutation. The Sami material reports two practitioners who set a period, named in advance the sign they demanded, received nothing and destroyed the instrument without explaining the failure away; it does not refute, because it reports conduct and not a rule of the transmission, and the node itself flags that both cases stand in a missionary's chapter on conversion. The Zande material prescribes a corroborative second test whose contradiction voids the verdict, which is an outcome a further run can establish and looks at first like the counter-case; it does not refute either, because the same transmission carries the eight causes beside it, so that the internal check and the external excuse coexist. What that pair shows is worth more than a clean confirmation: a transmission can hold a real check and an unlimited excuse at once, and the check then bounds nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission of the kind described whose earliest witness names an outcome that would count against the operation and supplies no condition assigning a failed run to the operator, the person operated on, the material or a third party. One such case refutes it. The likeliest places to look are the transmissions this holding carries that already name an observable sign of success, since a sign that can be present can also be absent: the feather at the nostril of the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, the gold-coloured body of the golden liquor, and the ten-day term of the medical canon."@en ;
    skos:note "The form was described from the outside, at the same period as two of the witnesses, by a first-century critic whose account this holding carries at pmc:BiansuiDispute: he put a counter-example to a practitioner three times and was answered three times with a fresh condition. Seeing the same form printed as instruction at pmc:JinchengYaocheng is what made the generalization worth forming. The description from outside is not counted among the witnesses, because a critic reporting the move is not a transmission supplying it."@en .
